Complete End-to-End CI/CD Pipeline for MERN Stack on Kubernetes

This guide will walk you through deploying your MERN application on a self-managed Kubernetes cluster using Jenkins, Docker, and GitHub, all on EC2 instances without using ECR or EKS.

Architecture Overview

text
GitHub → Jenkins (CI) → Docker Build → Push to Docker Hub → Kubernetes (CD) → Application Access
Prerequisites

AWS account with EC2 access
GitHub account with your MERN code
Docker Hub account (free tier works)
Domain name (optional, for production)
Step 1: Set Up EC2 Instances

You'll need three EC2 instances running Ubuntu 22.04 or 20.04:

Instance	Type	Purpose	Ports to Open
Jenkins Server	t2.medium (minimum)	CI/CD automation	8080 (Jenkins), 22 (SSH)
Kubernetes Master	t2.large	Control plane + workloads	6443, 80, 443, 22
Kubernetes Worker	t2.medium	Application pods	All ports from master
Launch Instructions:

Go to EC2 Dashboard → Launch Instance
Choose Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (HVM)
Select instance type as specified above
Configure security groups with the ports listed
Create/use a key pair for SSH access
Launch instances
Step 2: Install Docker on All Instances

Run these commands on all three EC2 instances:

bash
# Update system
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install Docker
sudo apt install docker.io -y

# Start and enable Docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

# Add current user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Apply group changes
newgrp docker

# Verify installation
docker --version
Step 3: Set Up Kubernetes Cluster Using Kubeadm

On Kubernetes Master Node:

bash
# Install kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl

# Add Kubernetes signing key
sudo curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.31/deb/Release.key | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg

# Add Kubernetes repository
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.31/deb/ /' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

# Install kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl

# Initialize the cluster
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

# Set up kubeconfig for regular user
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

# Install Flannel pod network
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml

# Remove master taint to run workloads on master node (for single-node setup)
kubectl taint nodes --all node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane-
kubectl taint nodes --all node-role.kubernetes.io/master-

# Generate join command for worker node
kubeadm token create --print-join-command

On Kubernetes Worker Node:

bash
# Install kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl (same commands as master)
# Then join the cluster using the command from master
sudo kubeadm join <master-ip>:6443 --token <token> --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<hash>
Verify Cluster on Master:

bash
kubectl get nodes
# Should show both master and worker nodes as Ready
